/** @page tarerrorsguide Error Handling

    @tableofcontents

    All TAR operations throw exceptions of type \c "TAR-ERROR" when errors occur:

    @code{.py}
try {
    TarFile tar("nonexistent.tar", "r");
} catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
    if (ex.err == "TAR-ERROR") {
        printf("TAR error: %s\n", ex.desc);
    }
}
    @endcode

    An archive entry header that cannot be read completely also raises \c "TAR-ERROR", from every operation
    that reads past it (entries(), entryCount(), lookups, reads, streams and extraction): a damaged header, or
    an entry whose name cannot be read.  An archive is never silently treated as ending at such an entry, so
    a listing or an extraction is either complete or fails.

    Path traversal attacks are detected and rejected with \c "TAR-SECURITY-ERROR":

    @code{.py}
# Archives containing paths like "../../../etc/passwd" are rejected
try {
    tar.extractAll(<TarExtractOptions>{"destination": "/safe/dir"});
} catch (hash<ExceptionInfo> ex) {
    if (ex.err == "TAR-SECURITY-ERROR") {
        printf("Blocked path traversal: %s\n", ex.desc);
    }
}
    @endcode
*/
